### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### REFERENCE — PUZZLE DATA SHAPES
###
### RECORDED, NOT EXECUTABLE. Everything below is the `data` payload of the three puzzle types, as
### returned by GET /v1/packs/{date} above. Read once before writing a client.
###
### A goFigure puzzle, with the three-rung hint ladder every one of them now carries:
###
###   { "data": {
###       "acceptedSolutions": ["6+7+9*7", "6+9+7*7", "7+6+9*7", "7+9+6*7", "9+6+7*7", "9+7+6*7"],
###       "bank": [6, 9, 7, 7],
###       "goal": 154,
###       "hints": [
###         { "operator": "+", "slot": 1 },
###         { "operator": "+", "slot": 0 },
###         { "operator": "*", "slot": 2 }
###       ],
###       "operatorTuples": [["+", "+", "*"]],
###       "operators": ["+", "-", "*", "/"]
###     },
###     "difficulty": 4,
###     "estimatedSeconds": 150,
###     "id": "2026-08-20:gofigure:abc123de",
###     "type": "gofigure" }
###
### That is the original game's own puzzle and a real difficulty-4 board: one operator tuple (++*)
### across six expressions, which is what rates it a 4.
###
### `hints` is REQUIRED and always exactly three rungs, so no read site needs to branch on its
### absence. Each rung names ONE operator slot of ONE canonical operator tuple, and the three are
### jointly satisfiable -- some accepted solution answers to all of them at once.
###
### A rung carries TWO fields and nothing derived from them. THE CLIENT WRITES THE SENTENCE.
###
###   `slot`     is the 0-based operator index, left to right, so `slot: 2` is the rightmost
###              operator, the one a player would call op3. What a board does with it is the board's
###              business -- there is no cell index here, because lull-ui renders the working
###              expression as one joined string and has no per-token cell.
###   `operator` is ASCII (+ - * /), matching `data.operators`. Map it to the board glyph yourself:
###              + − × ÷ is U+002B, U+2212, U+00D7, U+00F7. Note that three of those are NOT the
###              ASCII character -- '-' is not U+2212 and '*' is certainly not 'x'.
###
### There is no `kind` discriminator: a rung carrying `operator` IS an operator rung. There is no
### `text` either -- it was removed, because every rung's wording is a function of these two fields
### plus `operatorTuples`, and shipping the sentence meant shipping the same fact twice.
###
### The ladder ALWAYS ends on the rightmost operator, which is the strongest reveal: with the goal
### known it fixes the last step arithmetically, and on a * or / it names the final digit too. The
### first two rungs are ordered by difficulty, and on difficulties 4 and 5 the order is deliberately
### NOT least-to-most-revealing -- rung 1 there reveals op2, which is why the example above opens on
### slot 1 and closes on slot 2. An ordinal you print names the SLOT's position, never the rung's.
### Render the rungs in the order they arrive.
###
### THE WORDING, which lull-ui composes and which is recorded here so a second client matches it.
### Hedge when `operatorTuples.length > 1` -- more than one operator arrangement reaches the goal, so
### an unqualified claim about "the 1st operator" would assert a uniqueness that does not hold:
###
###   hedged, rung 1     One winning answer has "−" as its 1st operator.
###   hedged, rungs 2-3  The same answer has "×" as its 2nd operator.
###   unhedged, all      The 2nd operator from the left is "×".
###
### "The same answer" is copy, not a claim that one expression was pinned -- a dozen expressions can
### share a tuple. It stops a reader taking the three rungs for three different solutions.
###
### The glyph is QUOTED in every rung. A bare U+2212 with a space either side is hard to tell from an
### em dash, so an unquoted rung reads as a sentence that broke off.
###
### DO NOT RENUMBER, and keep "from the left" in the unhedged band. The rungs are ordered by how much
### each reveals, not left to right, so the one-tuple puzzles name their positions in the order 2, 1,
### 3. Anchoring the ordinal to the board is what stops it colliding with lull-ui's hint bar, which
### renders opened rungs into an ordered, decimal-marked list -- rung 1 would otherwise read "1. The
### 2nd operator is ×", two numbering systems disagreeing on one line. The hedged band needs no
### anchor: its ordinal hangs off "its", and its slots run 0, 1, 2 so marker and ordinal agree. That
### pairing holds because the 2, 1, 3 order and the unhedged copy are both the one-tuple case.
###
### NOTE the shape difference from the phrase types, which is the thing a shared hint renderer trips
### over: goFigure's `hints` is three OBJECTS, while Missing Vowels' and Cryptogram's is three plain
### STRINGS. A reader that assumes strings gets [object Object] three times.
###
### `operatorTuples` is every DISTINCT operator arrangement reaching the goal, deduplicated and
### sorted, never empty. It is derivable from `acceptedSolutions` by stripping the digits, and ships
### anyway: that strip is only correct because bank digits are 1-9 and every operand is therefore one
### character, and no client should have to know that. Its LENGTH is what the hint copy above hedges
### on, and it is exactly equivalent to difficulty -- one tuple is a difficulty 4 or 5, more than one
### is a 1, 2 or 3 -- but read the tuples, not the difficulty, so the rule survives a re-banding.
###
### `operators` is the PALETTE the board offers, always all four, and not the solution's operators.
### `acceptedSolutions` is every expression string reaching the goal, sorted, never empty, and
### evaluated strictly LEFT TO RIGHT with no operator precedence (6+7+9*7 is 154, not 76). Each bank
### digit is used exactly once. The device adjudicates locally against this list; the answer ships
### with the pack because the pack is already on the phone.
###
### The two phrase-backed types:
###
###   missingvowels -> { "answer", "category"?, "displayed", "hints": ["...", "...", "..."] }
###   cryptogram    -> { "answer", "category"?, "ciphertext", "hints": ["...", "...", "..."] }
###
### `answer` ships for the same offline-first reason `acceptedSolutions` does.
###
### `category` is OMITTED, not null, wherever it is hidden -- hiding it is part of the difficulty.
### The table hides it at difficulties 3 AND 5 (generators/category-visibility.ts), but ON THE WIRE
### only difficulty 3 ever loses it: no phrase type generates a 5. Missing Vowels runs [1, 2, 3, 4]
### and Cryptogram runs [2, 3, 4]. So expect `category` on every phrase puzzle except the 3s, and do
### not read the table's 5 as something you will see.
